@dari-us maybe `params.expect_only` that would raise.

The existing configuration makes sense. Logging can be useful in dev but would be extra noise in most production applications. This implies to me that logging is an environment config and raising should be controlled by how the methods are called. Please correct me if you think otherwise.

If we added `expect_only` and `permit_only` (for a non-require version) that would allow the use case in #26831. These would be methods that would raise `UnpermittedParameters`. `expect` and `permit` would continue to log or raise depending on the global config, allowing an app that wants both to set globally, and use the `_only` methods selectively. I suggest doing this in a follow-up PR that I can certainly help with.

@martinemde
You could copy/paste the three files I changed in main, they are all passing.
You can then git diff to view my changes compared to your code ^^

@martinemde Agreed, seeing as the explicit Array filter solves another class of `NoMethodError` caused by param-tampering, it should definitely be the default at some point and the old behavior should become deprecated.
The code looks really good.

Is this ready for final review or is anything still missing?

@dari-us Thank you for the response. I updated the description a little, though I was doing it on my phone at the time so I'll make another pass at it now.

@MatheusRich, @dhh, let me know what you think and if there's anywhere that we should update. I think it's also possible to split the `_only` methods out of this PR.

I think the explicit array behavior is absolutely necessary with this PR because `expect(user: [:name])` would otherwise  open us up to a variation of the original problem I was trying to solve: you could pass an array `{ "user" => [{ "name" => "me" }] }` (aka `?user[][name]=me`) in place of the expected hash (aka `?user[name]=me`), breaking the type contract that I was trying to fix.

EDIT: I note that there is one failing test that doesn't look related. Someone more familiar with this test suite could please let me know if this is actually something I need to look into.

@martinemde I think we can take multiple stabs at this. Most important is to get the new #expect syntax in place, and to have that start generating 400s using a new public/400-bad-request.html template that's automatically configured by ActionController::Base using rescue_from.

Remind me of point 3?

For point 4, I'd love to have expect! as a way to transition off `action_on_unpermitted_parameters`. I think that top-level toggle was a mistake, and going forward, it should be clear at the call-site what's going to happen.

@dhh, I think it covers everything except the cases we were aiming to fix with `allow` or more specific cases where you don't want the keys required.

I think the simplest way to get this merged is to separate the `permit!` behavior into a now PR, which I did here in #52737. This lays all the groundwork for `expect` and `expect!` and covers the use cases where permit would still be useful. It makes it immediately possible to ignore the `action_on_unpermitted_parameters` and use the new API. I see this as a separate issue compared to what expect is trying to solve.

I'm going to build the narrowed `expect` and `expect!` on top of that commit, constraining the `[[:key]]` pattern only to expect for now. I wrote it such that it's easy to switch later if we want to make it a rails version upgrade setting.

@martinemde
A few more nitpicks, as of the above two review comments, also see my reasoning in regards to renaming kwarg `check_unpermitted` to `on_unpermitted` across all methods. Instead of calling with `false` from `expect`, using `on_unpermitted: nil` would be sensible then, as in "on unpermitted parameters do nothing". That is in line with the alternate values `:log` and `:raise`.

We have reached nitpick territory at this point, but I'm a perfectionist 😅

@p8 I hardly see that as a problem, at least I cannot remember ever seeing `except` used with `params`.
`allow` sounds less strict, i.e. I wouldn't expect it to raise for missing root keys.
I would love to have `allow` as a replacement for `params.fetch(:x, {}).permit(...)` sometime, as this pattern appears in the docs, I often use it for "filter forms" and is not fixed by the `expect` API.

