@rafaelfranca I think I found the issue you mean: https://github.com/rails/rails/issues/42953

It was marked stale, but apparently there are some PRs and suggestions how people think it should be fixed.

I believe adding a new method that is used instead of `require` when nested params are expected (and consistently using that in the docs for these situations) is the most simple, safe and compatible way to go. I based my `nested` on `fetch`, because it allows for a fallback.
